    "narrative": "The core of our lineup today is anchored by **Josh Naylor** (68.45) and **Isaac Paredes** (68.21), who both benefit from favorable matchups against Nick Martinez and Cal Quantrill, respectively. **Naylor** leverages a 19% SP skill advantage, while **Paredes** enjoys a significant platoon boost against right-handed pitching. Despite the high-caliber presence of Shohei Ohtani on the mound, **Seiya Suzuki** (66.11) remains our most effective offensive engine; his ability to exploit Hunter Greene\u2019s profile in a park environment providing a 3% boost pushes him to the top of our daily hierarchy. **Jose Caballero** (65.01) is another priority start, as his 100% stolen base opportunity profile against Carson Palmquist remains essential for our league-leading pursuit of speed, even against a relatively tough 3.57 xERA.\n\nThe decision to bench **Austin Riley** (57.16) was our most agonizing margin call, as his score sits just points away from the active tier. While **Riley** offers reliable pop, his utility was suppressed by a lower batting order slot (assumed #7) and a modest penalty due to the opposing pitcher\u2019s ability to hold runners, a metric we weight heavily in our 5x5 framework. Conversely, we are keeping faith in **Mickey Moniak** (62.51) and **Kyle Karros** (63.20) in the outfield and UTIL spots, respectively. Even though both face a tough test in Robbie Ray, they benefit from a \"wind out\" scenario and a high-leverage batting order position (#2), which provides them a ceiling that our bench options currently lack. \n\nCaution is warranted across the board as the majority of our lineup is currently \"Lineup Pending.\" Specifically, **Corbin Carroll** (59.94) and **Gabriel Moreno** (47.31) face the stiffest resistance of the day against Ohtani. While **Carroll**'s speed-first profile makes him a mandatory start regardless of the matchup, **Moreno** is skating on thin ice; if his official order drops below the #4 spot, his efficiency score will likely dip into the marginal tier. We are monitoring the status of **James Wood** (55.03) closely as well\u2014while his 0.432 xwOBA makes him an elite talent, the matchup against Ryan Weathers presents a neutral platoon floor that demands we keep him locked in the lineup despite the risk of elite pitching.",
